,'dogtimemedia.com','guest9317','2008-10-15 16:03:28','ok so i just type medicine for cat hairball'),(11319,'dogtimemedia.com','guest1706','2008-10-15 16:03:32','so it might be good to check that out in lieu of going to the vet if you can\\\'t at this point'),(11320,'dogtimemedia.com','guest1706','2008-10-15 16:03:42','yes, i just typed \\\"cat hairball medicine\\\"'),(11321,'dogtimemedia.com','guest1706','2008-10-15 16:04:38','here\\\'s something I just found:  Hairballs are an extremely common problem seen in cats causing vomiting.  When a cat licks himself with his rough tongue to clean his beautiful fur coat, he will often pick up pieces of shed fur on his rough tongue and swallow them.  The problem with hair is that it does not digest but, rather, tends to collect in the catâ€™s stomach and form a large wad of hair---the â€œhairballâ€.  This wad of hair tends to act as an unwanted valve within the stomach, getting in the way of normal digestion and may also irritate the lining of the stomach and esophagus (as it is swallowed), and can cause irritation to the stomach and vomiting.'),(11322,'dogtimemedia.com','guest9317','2008-10-15 16:06:02','oh my poor thing'),(11323,'dogtimemedia.com','guest1706','2008-10-15 16:06:05','hope that helps!'),(11324,'dogtimemedia.com','guest9317','2008-10-15 16:06:26','thanks alot yes i will show this to my husband'),(11325,'dogtimemedia.com','guest1706','2008-10-15 16:06:29','every cat i\\\'ve had my whole life has done that'),(11326,'dogtimemedia.com','guest1706','2008-10-15 16:06:38','because of hairballs'),(11327,'dogtimemedia.com','guest9317','2008-10-15 16:06:42','ours licks himself alot'),(11328,'dogtimemedia.com','guest1706','2008-10-15 16:07:37','that\\\'s probably it'),(11329,'dogtimemedia.com','guest3567','2008-10-15 16:23:17','Hey'),(11330,'dogtimemedia.com','guest7194','2008-10-15 16:28:34','the other thing to do is brush your cat once a day, it gets rid of the some of the excess hair that cause the furballs'),(11331,