d will not be able to participate. Please join us in an enjoyable chat and always visit us again hosted events and other fun information.â€'),(24368,'dogtimemedia.com','guest5857','2008-11-10 22:23:32','hi!'),(24369,'dogtimemedia.com','guest5857','2008-11-10 22:27:26','is it true, chocolates are not good for dogs?'),(24370,'dogtimemedia.com','guest357','2008-11-10 22:33:26','My Beagle is too fat (36.2 lbs).  The vet recently did a thyroid panel and says he has an underactive thyroid so he is on thyroid medication now.  Anyone else have this problem with their Beagle?'),(24371,'dogtimemedia.com','guest5857','2008-11-10 22:37:32','Autoimmune Thyroiditis: This is the most common cause of primary hypothyroidism in dogs and is recognized as a heritable condition. The disease tends to become evident at two to five years of age. Dogs may be clinically normal for years, only to become hypothyroid at a later date. Hypothyroidism is an abnormally low level of the hormone produced by the thyroid gland. A mild sign of the disease may be infertility. More obvious signs include obesity, mental dullness, drooping of the eyelids, low energy levels, and irregular heat cycles. The dog\\\'s fur becomes coarse and brittle and begins to fall out, while the skin becomes tough and dark. Hypothyroidism can be treated with daily medication, which must continue throughout the dog\\\'s life. A dog receiving daily thyroid treatment can live a full and happy life.'),(24372,'dogtimemedia.com','guest942','2008-11-10 22:46:56','aloha!'),(24373,'dogtimemedia.com','pojo','2008-11-10 22:50:35','Thank you for the information. My Beagle just turned 4 yrs old in May, 2008-while he is a little on the chubby side-he doesn\\\'t have any of the other symptoms-the only reason I found out is because he had a neck injury that dispite medication and a trip to the vet-wouldn\\\'t heal-so the vet suspected his weight may have something to do with it so she decided to run the thyroid panel (we had already tried the diet thing-which didn\\\'t work either).  BTW: Chocolate is not good for dogs so please don\\\'t feed it to your dog-pet stores carry carob treats for dogs-they won\\\'t know the difference.'),(24374,'dogtimemedia.com','guest6326','2008-11-10 22:54:55','Thanks for the info on chocolates. Hope ur dog will get well soon.'),(24375,'dogtimemedia.com','pojo','2008-11-10 22:56:52','Thanks, he seems to be doing ok (he should be-he\\\'s on a bunch of meds)and I bought him a new bed and spoil him rotten-LOL!  Of course, then I had to buy a new bed for my other dog too so he wouldn\\\'t feel left out! The things we do for our pets-go figure!'),(24376,'dogtimemedia.com','guest6326','2008-11-10 22:59:47','lol.. that\\\'s a good idea!'),(24377,'dogtimemedia.com','DogTimeMediaMVN2','2008-11-10 23:01:30','â€œWe highly encourage all guest users to sign up for a username. You can do so by clicking on â€œsignupâ€ right underneath this box. Once you register, PetChat community will be able to recognize you and weâ€™ll also send you email newsletters of upcoming hosted events. Hosted events will include things like live QA with renown trainers, vets, and professionals where you can come visit and ask questions, learn, and have fun.â€'),(24378,'dogtimemedia.com','DogTimeMediaMVN2','2008-11-10 23:01:50','â€œDogTime Media focuses on providing the best chat experience with other pet enthusiasts for PetChat users. We have moderators logged in to ensure we can create that environment for you. Users who are saying offensive words/topics will be suspended from chat and will not be able to participate. Please join us in an enjoyable chat and always visit us again hosted events and other fun information.â€'),(24379,'dogtimemedia.com','guest6326','2008-11-10 23:02:47','how many dogs do you have pojo?'),(24380,'dogtimemedia.com','pojo','2008-11-10 23:10:47','I have 2 now. I had 3 but I had to take one of them to the shelter cuz he\\\'s the one that attacked my Beagle and hurt his neck.  One 